rt 8080\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A Python stdlib HTTP server was deployed under systemd (inventory.service, Restart=always) on both node2 and node3, bound to 0.0.0.0:8080, firewalled for 8080/tcp, and verified serving JSON inventory data with correct backend hostname both immediately after setup and after each node's mandatory restart. The after-executor lifecycle snapshot independently confirms python3 listening on 0.0.0.0:8080 on both node2 and node3, with no failed services.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-eade16c7e0f74068\",\"cmd-d1482ee3f2d24648\",\"cmd-1e29bb12535c4f3b\",\"cmd-6d0396809d844223\",\"cmd-c2d824036af34bd8\",\"cmd-437ba39989d64c84\",\"global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node1 load-balances incoming HTTP requests across both backends on port 80\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"nginx was installed and configured on node1 with an upstream block containing both backend IPs on 8080 and a proxy_pass in the port-80 server block; SELinux boolean and firewalld http service were opened to permit this. nginx -t validated the config after a self-corrected duplicate-block error, and repeated curls to localhost:80 and $NODE_IP:80 show alternating responses from node2 and node3, both before and after node1's own restart and after node2/node3 restarts. The after-executor snapshot confirms nginx listening on 0.0.0.0:80 with no failed services.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a324910d212b44c6\",\"cmd-d37e8c1fb8444c10\",\"cmd-a1ac9088ea034f15\",\"cmd-041c8df7cb374885\",\"cmd-41827092c9994d4c\",\"cmd-b4d81dfe915a4e6f\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"One bounded, self-corrected mutation occurred: cmd-002e3c99ff3e4479 appended a duplicate location/proxy_pass block into /etc/nginx/nginx.conf via sed, which broke nginx -t syntax validation (return_code 1). This was immediately diagnosed and fixed by cmd-d37e8c1fb8444c10 (a python script removing the duplicate block), after which nginx -t passed and the config was proven functional. The mutation was low-risk (a config file, not data or security controls), quickly detected via the same validation loop, and fully corrected within the same session, leaving no broken residue in the after-executor snapshot. All other state-changing commands (nginx install, systemd unit/app creation on node2/node3, firewalld port/service additions, SELinux boolean, node restarts) map directly to material task outcomes or the mandated restart protocol. No abandoned files, orphaned services, or weakened security controls are visible in the after-executor snapshot; only expected /tmp/reboot.log and new boot-id-scoped systemd-private dirs appear, consistent with the sanctioned restarts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-002e3c99ff3e4479\",\"cmd-d37e8c1fb8444c10\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task — inventory app on node2/node3:8080 and nginx round-robin load balancing on node1:80 — are directly and repeatedly demonstrated via curl tests, including after each node's restart. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was also executed cleanly on all three nodes with matching pre/post boot IDs against the fixed lifecycle snapshots, recovered services, and even a captured mid-outage failover to node3 while node2 was rebooting. Hygiene is only lightly impacted by one bounded, self-corrected nginx.conf editing mistake.\",\"limitations\":[\"Restart-recovery evidence for node1 relies on nginx being 'active' and LB curls succeeding, but no independent check of node1's own systemd failed-units list was captured immediately post-restart (only the later after-executor snapshot, which is clean).\",\"The nginx config approach (placing location/proxy_pass directly in the main nginx.conf server block rather than a self-contained conf.d server block) is functionally validated but is an inelegant deviation from typical modular practice; this is a style observation, not a defect, since nginx -t and live curls confirm correctness.\",\"Package-health observations only report 'manager=dnf' without underlying consistency-check output, so package state cleanliness could not be independently verified beyond the presence/absence of failed services.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Inventory web application running on node2 and node3, listening on port 8080","status":"satisfied","summary":"A Python stdlib HTTP server was deployed under systemd (inventory.service, Restart=always) on both node2 and node3, bound to 0.0.0.0:8080, firewalled for 8080/tcp, and verified serving JSON inventory data with correct backend hostname both immediately after setup and after each node's mandatory restart. The after-executor lifecycle snapshot independently confirms python3 listening on 0.0.0.0:8080 on both node2 and node3, with no failed services.","evidence_ids":["cmd-eade16c7e0f74068","cmd-d1482ee3f2d24648","cmd-1e29bb12535c4f3b","cmd-6d0396809d844223","cmd-c2d824036af34bd8","cmd-437ba39989d64c84","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node3:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"node1 load-balances incoming HTTP requests across both backends on port 80","status":"satisfied","summary":"nginx was installed and configured on node1 with an upstream block containing both backend IPs on 8080 and a proxy_pass in the port-80 server block; SELinux boolean and firewalld http service were opened to permit this. nginx -t validated the config after a self-corrected duplicate-block error, and repe